+# Decision: Delivery approach for committed board changes
+
+## Context
+
+Editors submit commands over HTTPS and the server assigns each committed edit one authoritative event revision. Up to 800 staff browsers must see that revision reflected on their board, and the mechanism for getting it there shapes three design nodes at once: how the change store represents and exposes revision history, what delivery does with a committed revision, and how a browser keeps itself current and recovers after a disconnect. Because all three nodes size themselves around this choice, it is decided once, up front, rather than separately by each node.
+
+The project's freshness goal — committed changes visible within 15 seconds under normal connectivity — is met by every option on the table; it does not by itself separate them. The real tension is between update latency quality, who owns the operational burden of keeping 800 browsers current through bursts of 20 edits per second, and whether that operational burden is carried in-house or handed to a recurring-fee vendor.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Gates:**
+
+- Must support 800 simultaneous browsers and opening-time bursts of 20 committed edits per second (inherited — HLD Goals). All three options can be built to pass this; none is eliminated by it.
+- Must keep connectivity failures visible rather than letting a browser mistake silence for an unchanged board (inherited tenet — "Connectivity failures must be visible, not mistaken for an unchanged board"). All three options can satisfy this with the right design; it shapes each option's browser-side work rather than ruling one out.
+
+**Weighted criteria:**
+
+- **Update latency quality** (major). The Goals set a 15-second ceiling and separately note "subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required" — so sub-second delivery is a real but non-binding plus, not a tenet-backed requirement.
+- **Operational burden of connection capacity and reconnection** (major). Someone has to handle 800 concurrent connections (or poll cycles) and opening-time bursts; the question is whether that burden is absorbed by infrastructure the team runs, a vendor, or neither (stateless HTTP).
+- **Recurring cost and vendor coupling** (major). The tenet "Self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted" means neither is ruled out on policy grounds — but it leaves open how heavily a per-connection or per-message vendor bill, and dependence on that vendor's API and reliability, should weigh against the alternative of owning the infrastructure directly.
+- **Added complexity to the store, delivery, and browser nodes** (moderate). Each option pushes different work onto each node; this shapes how those nodes' LLDs will read, but doesn't by itself favor one option over another.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+Browsers issue a periodic HTTP request — every five seconds — asking the change store for revisions committed since their last-known cursor. Delivery stays request/response: there is no held connection and no server-initiated push. A reconnecting browser simply resumes polling from its last cursor.
+
+- **Latency quality**: partial fit. Up to five seconds of polling interval plus request latency before a change is seen — within the 15-second ceiling, but the furthest from the sub-second plus the Goals mention as useful-but-not-required.
+- **Operational burden**: strong fit. Nothing stateful to run — no connection pool, no reconnect storms, no session lifecycle. A failed poll is indistinguishable from a successful no-op poll and simply retries next cycle.
+- **Cost/vendor coupling**: strong fit. No vendor and no new infrastructure beyond the existing HTTP serving path, though 800 browsers polling every five seconds is a standing read load the store must size for.
+- **Node complexity**: partial fit. The change store must support efficient cursor queries ("revisions since X"); delivery and the browser stay close to their simplest shape.
+- **Summary**: the least operational commitment, at the cost of continuous read load and the most staleness of the three.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+The server holds a persistent connection per connected browser. Delivery pushes each committed revision to subscribed connections as it commits, rather than waiting to be asked. The change store keeps revision history replayable so a browser that reconnects after a gap can request everything it missed and catch up to the latest state.
+
+- **Latency quality**: strong fit. Revisions are pushed as they commit; updates are sub-second under normal connectivity.
+- **Operational burden**: weak fit. The team owns connection capacity for up to 800 concurrent sockets, opening-time connection bursts, and reconnect handling when a browser's socket drops — this is capacity and failure-mode work with no vendor absorbing it.
+- **Cost/vendor coupling**: strong fit. No recurring per-connection or per-message fee, and no dependence on a third party's API shape or uptime for the board's core realtime path.
+- **Node complexity**: partial fit. The change store must make revisions replayable and publish each one reliably to delivery; delivery owns socket session lifecycle (accept, authorize, maintain, push); the browser carries reconnect-and-catch-up logic to detect a dropped connection and request replay rather than assume nothing changed.
+- **Summary**: fast, push-based updates with no vendor bill or provider dependency, paid for by owning connection capacity and reconnection handling directly.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+A third-party realtime/pub-sub provider terminates the browser connections. Delivery wraps the provider's publish API instead of managing sockets itself; the change store reconciles its own committed revisions with what has been published to the provider's channels.
+
+- **Latency quality**: strong fit. Sub-second delivery, typically backed by infrastructure purpose-built and tuned for this.
+- **Operational burden**: strong fit. The provider owns connection scaling, geographic distribution, and reconnect handling; the team's operational surface shrinks to integration code.
+- **Cost/vendor coupling**: weak fit. Recurring per-connection or per-message fees that scale with the 800-browser, 20-edit-per-second load, and the board's realtime path becomes dependent on a specific provider's API, pricing, and reliability.
+- **Node complexity**: partial fit. The change store needs publication reconciliation against the provider; delivery is a thin wrapper around the provider's API; the browser adopts the provider's subscription model rather than a custom one.
+- **Summary**: the quickest route to low-latency updates with the least in-house connection work, in exchange for a recurring bill and coupling the board's core path to a specific vendor.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Self-hosted WebSockets.** Update latency quality is a major criterion, and both self-hosted WebSockets and the hosted realtime service satisfy it equally well — polling is the only option that trails here, and the margin (seconds of staleness, still within the 15-second ceiling) was judged not worth paying for by itself since sub-second delivery is explicitly a "useful, not required" plus. That leaves WebSockets and the hosted service to be separated on the other two major criteria, and they pull in opposite directions: the hosted service wins on operational burden, WebSockets wins on cost and vendor coupling. No tenet forces this either way — "Self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted" leaves both in contention — but keeping the board's core realtime path independent of a realtime vendor's recurring fees and API surface was weighed as the deciding major criterion, and the operational cost of owning connection capacity and reconnection handling was accepted as the trade for it.
+
+This commits the three downstream nodes: the change store must expose replayable revision history and publish each committed revision reliably; delivery owns WebSocket session lifecycle end to end (accepting, authorizing, maintaining, and pushing over connections it operates); browser sync carries reconnect-and-catch-up logic rather than a stateless subscription model. It forecloses, for this project, routing delivery through a polling-based cursor API or a hosted provider's SDK without reopening this decision.
